  "Question": "For one root of \\(ax^2+bx+c=0\\) to be double the other, the coefficients \\(a,\\) \\(b,\\) \\(c\\) must be related as follows:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4b^2=9c\\)",
    "B": "\\(2b^2=9ac\\)",
    "C": "\\(2b^2=9a\\)",
    "D": "\\(b^2-8ac=0\\)",
    "E": "\\(9b^2=2ac\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "For a quadratic, \\(a\\ne0\\). Write the roots as \\(r\\) and \\(2r\\). Their factored form reveals how the root relationship affects the coefficients:\n\\[\na(x-r)(x-2r)\n=ax^2-3arx+2ar^2.\n\\]\nComparing with \\(ax^2+bx+c\\) gives \\(b=-3ar\\) and \\(c=2ar^2\\). Eliminating \\(r\\),\n\\[\n2b^2=2(9a^2r^2)=18a^2r^2=9ac.\n\\]\nThis relation also suffices. If \\(2b^2=9ac\\), set \\(r=-b/(3a)\\). Then \\(b=-3ar\\) and\n\\[\nc=\\frac{2b^2}{9a}=2ar^2,\n\\]\nso the quadratic is \\(a(x-r)(x-2r)\\). The calculation includes \\(r=0\\), giving two zero roots, because no division by a root was used. The required relation is \\(\\boxed{2b^2=9ac}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
